\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Models and benchmarks.} gpt-oss-20b and gpt-oss-120b \citep{openai2025gptoss} and
Gemma-4-26B-A4B-it \citep{google2026gemma4}, a mixture-of-experts model. $L_0$ uses each model's cheapest
reasoning setting (low effort for gpt-oss, thinking off for Gemma) with greedy decoding; $L_1$ and the
pipeline use the default setting (medium effort, thinking on) at temperature 1.0. We evaluate on MATH-500
\citep{hendrycks2021math,lightman2024verify}, FRAMES \citep{krishna2025frames}, answerable MuSiQue
\citep{trivedi2022musique} and BIG-Bench Extra Hard (BBEH; \citealp{kazemi2025bbeh}): test splits of 400,
660 and 1{,}934 tasks for the first three, whose other 20\% served to build the pipeline, and 874 BBEH
problems (38 from each of its 23 subtasks), which played no part in it. FRAMES and MuSiQue are open-book: every call receives
the question's documents (median 17k and 2.4k tokens), so every rung pays for them. MATH is scored by
symbolic equivalence, BBEH by its official fuzzy match, and FRAMES and MuSiQue by an LLM judge
(gpt-oss-120b). Cost is prompt plus completion tokens, including hidden reasoning and tool calls, over all
calls a task makes. Serving, documents, scoring and generation caps are detailed in
Appendix~\ref{app:details}.

\paragraph{Records and replay.} For every task we record the reference pipeline and \climb{}'s ladder
forced through every cheap rung, which gives the outcome, cost and confidence of $L_0$ and $L_1$.
Replaying these records evaluates any policy on any subset of tasks; an escalating task receives the
reference's outcome and pays its cost on top of the rungs already run.

\paragraph{Protocol and baselines.} Losses are measured against the reference, and tolerances are
absolute: $\eps = 10\%$ allows a loss of 10 accuracy points. We draw 200 random calibration/evaluation
splits (50/50; 100 splits of 20/40/40 for policies that need training data) and report means; the \emph{violation
rate} is the share of splits in which the selected policy's loss on the full pool exceeds $\eps$. We
compare with uncertified rules (always stopping at $L_0$ or at $L_1$, and a single confidence threshold,
fixed across settings, that spends as much as \climb{}), an oracle that stops at the cheapest correct rung, and richer escalation
policies certified as \climb{} is (Appendix~\ref{sec:variants}).
